          %h2#Idea Idea
          %p
            Equivariant elliptic cohomology is, or is supposed to be, an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivariant+cohomology"} equivariant
            version of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/elliptic+cohomology"} elliptic cohomology
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/chromatic+homotopy+theory"} chromatic
            analogue of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivariant+K-theory"} equivariant K-theory
          %p
            As usual in
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivariant+cohomology"} equivariant cohomology
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+topology"} algebraic topology
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+theory"} homotopy theory
            are indicated below in
          %ul
            %li
              %em
                %a{:href => "#MotivationFromAlgebraicTopology"} Motivation from algebraic topology
          %p
            Despite that motivation, the precise nature of the resulting “genuine” equivariant elliptic cohomology may tend to seem a bit mysterious and also a bit baroque in its technical ingredients, some of which may appear a bit unexpected in the literature. A clear conceptual picture of what equivariant elliptic cohomology is about is obtained by regarding it as encoding aspects of low dimensional
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+field+theory"} quantum field theory
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/worldsheet"} worldsheet
            = succeed ";" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+theory"} string theory
          %ul
            %li
              %em>
                %a{:href => "#InterpretationInQuantumFieldTheory"} Interpretation in Quantum field theory/String theory
              \.
          %h3#MotivationFromAlgebraicTopology Motivation from algebraic topology
          %p
            Given any
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cohomology+theory"} cohomology theory
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi E
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E
            which may be evaluated on arbitrary
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+spaces"} topological spaces
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/compact+Lie+group"} compact Lie group
            the “naive”
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivariant+cohomology"} equivariant E-cohomology
            of the point is the
            = succeed "-cohomology" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi E
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classifying+space"} classifying space
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi B
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B G
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            (which is equivalently the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/delooping"} delooping
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi B
                  %mi G
                  %mo ≃
                  %mo *
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  B G \simeq \ast //G
          %p
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            regarded as an
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9E-group"} ∞-group
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9E-action"} ∞-action
            for how that encodes actions on structures above it):
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msubsup
                    %mi E
                    %mi G
                    %mo •
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo *
                  %msub
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mi naive
                  %mo ≔
                  %msup
                    %mi E
                    %mo •
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi B
                  %mi G
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  E_G^\bullet(\ast)_{naive} \coloneqq E^\bullet(B G)
                  \\,.
          %p
            In a discussion in the context of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+homotopy+theory"} geometric homotopy theory
            it is clear what is “naive” about this definition: since
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            has geometric structure of which
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi B
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B G
            remembers only the underlying
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometrically+discrete+infinity-groupoid"} bare homotopy type
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+stack"} smooth stack
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi B
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{B}G
            (the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/moduli+stack"} moduli stack
            of
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            = succeed ")," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/principal+bundle"} principal bundle
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi E
                    %mo •
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi B
                  %mi G
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{E}^\bullet(\mathbf{B}G)
            where now
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi E
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{E}
            is some
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sheaf+of+spectra"} sheaf of spectra
            and then declare this to be the actual
            = succeed "-equivariant" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            = succeed "-cohomology." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi E
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E
          %p
            The traditional argument however proceeds as follows: if
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi E
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complex+oriented+cohomology+theory"} complex oriented cohomology theory
            then (essentially by definition) for
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                  %mo =
                  %mi U
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G = U(1)
            the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/circle+group"} circle group
            then
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi E
                    %mo •
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi B
                  %mi U
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ≃
                  %msup
                    %mi E
                    %mo •
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo *
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %msubsup
                    %mi c
                    %mn 1
                    %mi E
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E^\bullet(B U(1)) \simeq E^\bullet(\ast)[ [ c_1^E ] ]
            is the algebra of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/formal+power+series"} formal power series
            which one may think of as the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebra+of+functions"} algebra of functions
            on the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/formal+geometry"} formal
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/neighbourhood"} neighbourhood
            of a point in some larger
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/space"} space
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi M
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mi S
                          %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M_{S^1}
          %p
            For instance in the simpler case of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivariant+K-theory"} equivariant K-theory
            this has long been well understood: here the genuine
            = succeed "-equivariant" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi U
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U(1)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/representation+ring"} representation ring
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi K
                    %mrow
                      %mi U
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo *
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ≃
                  %mi ℤ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %msup
                    %mi t
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                  %mi t
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K_{U(1)}(\ast) \simeq  \mathbb{Z}[ [ t^{-1}, t] ]
            which happens to be the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebra+of+functions"} algebra of functions
            on the
            = succeed ";" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/multiplicative+group"} multiplicative group
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complex+oriented+cohomology+theory"} complex orientation
            the naive equivariant cohomology
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi K
                    %mo •
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi B
                  %mi U
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ≃
                  %mi ℤ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi t
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K^\bullet(B U(1)) \simeq \mathbb{Z}[ [t] ]
            is equivalently the algebra of functions on (just) the formal multiplicative group.
          %p
            Based on this one may want to consider an
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi E
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-module+bundle"} ∞-line bundle
            over the full space
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi M
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi S
                        %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M_{S^1}
            and take the genuine
            = succeed "-equivariant" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi E
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/global+sections"} global sections
            of that. (Specifically in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/elliptic+cohomology"} elliptic cohomology
            that space
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi M
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi S
                        %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M_{S^1}
            is equivalent to the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/elliptic+curve"} elliptic curve
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            that gives the theory its name, but in some sense discussed
            %a{:href => "#InterpretationInQuantumFieldTheory"} below
            the spaces
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi M
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi S
                        %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M_{S^1}
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            arise conceptually differently and it is a fairly deep coincidence that they are in fact equivalent, which one may want to remember.)
          %p
            In this way equivariant elliptic cohomology was defined in (
            %a{:href => "#Grojnowski94"}> Grojnowski 94
            ,
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#GinzburgKapranovVasserot95"} Ginzburg-Kapranov-Vasserot 95
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#Ando00"} Ando 00, sections II.8, II.9
          %p
            More generally then genuine
            = succeed "-equivariant" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            = succeed "-action" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/space"} space
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            a sheaf
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℱ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{F}
            of algebras over the
            = succeed "-equivariant" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            = succeed "-equivariant" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            should be the global sections of this.
          %p
            While this can be made to work, it remains maybe unclear what these spaces
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi M
                    %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M_G
            “mean” and what makes them related to equivariance and elliptic cohomology. Specifically,
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi M
                    %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M_G
            turns out to be essentially the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/moduli+space+of+flat+connections"} moduli space of flat connections
            (
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            \-
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/principal+connections"}> principal connections
            ) on the given elliptic curve (see remark
            %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#ModuliSpaceOfFlatConnections"}
            below), which suggests strong relations to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Chern-Weil+theory"} Chern-Weil theory
            that are not apparent here. That is considerably clarified by regarding elliptic cohomology as the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coefficients"} coefficients
            for
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/motivic+quantization"} cohomological quantization
            of 3d and 2d
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+field+theory"} quantum field theory
          %h3#InterpretationInQuantumFieldTheory Interpretation in Quantum field theory/String theory
          %p
            We now try to give a maybe more conceptual explanation of what genuine equivariant (and
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/twisted+cohomology"} twisted
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/elliptic+curves"} elliptic curves
            (hence: “genuine equivariant twisted
            = succeed "\u201D)." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tmf"} tmf
          %p
            The conceptual role of plain
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/elliptic+cohomology"} elliptic cohomology
            (not equivariant) was considerably clarified when (
            %a{:href => "Witten+genus#Witten87"}> Witten 87
            ) identified the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/elliptic+genus"} elliptic genus
            (an element in the elliptic cohomology of a point) with the (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/large+volume+limit"} large volume limit
            of) the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/partition+function"} partition function
            of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2d+superconformal+field+theory"} 2d superconformal field theory
            – the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/worldsheet"} worldsheet
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+field+theory"} quantum field theory
            of the “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/superstring"}> superstring
            ” – where the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/worldsheet"} worldsheet
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Riemann+surface"} Riemann surface
            of the string is identified with the given
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/elliptic+curve"} elliptic curve
          %p
            If the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/superstring"} superstring
            here is specifically the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/heterotic+string"} heterotic string
            then its dynamics and hence its
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/partition+function"} partition function
            depends in general not just on the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/target+space"} target spacetime
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            (of which it yields the
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/elliptic+genus"} elliptic genus
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/background+field"} background
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+field"} gauge field
            for some
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+group"} gauge group
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/principal+bundle"} principal bundle
            over that spacetime. In (
            %a{:href => "Witten+genus#KL95"}> Kefeng Liu, 95
            ) a succinct description of these “twisted” elliptic genera, twisted by a
            = succeed "-principal" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kac-Weyl+characters"} Kac-Weyl characters
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/associated+bundle"} associated
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/loop+group"} loop group
            bundles. In (
            %a{:href => "#DistlerSharpe07"}> Distler-Sharpe 07
            ) the chiral
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/WZW-model"} WZW-model
            part of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/heterotic+string"} heterotic string
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2d+SCFT"} 2d SCFT
            which emobodies the effect of this background gauge bundle was realized geometrically as a bundle of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/parameterized+WZW+models"} parameterized WZW models
            over
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "#Ando07"} Ando 07
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "#DistlerSharpe07"} Distler-Sharpe 07, section 8.5
            = succeed ")," do
              %a{:href => "Witten+genus#KL95"} Kefeng Liu, 95
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "#Ando07"} Ando 07
            = succeed "-equivariant" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
          %p
            Now in the special case that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            here is the point, then any
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/parameterized+WZW+model"} parameterized WZW model
            over
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            is just the plain single
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/WZW+model"} WZW model
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Witten+genus"} Witten genus
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            vanishes. So in this case the interpretation of (
            %a{:href => "#Ando07"}> Ando 07
            ) says that the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/partition+function"} partition function
            of the
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/WZW+model"} WZW model
            should be an element in the
            = succeed "-equivariant" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/conformal+blocks"} conformal blocks
            of the WZW-model over a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/torus"} torus
            worldsheet, hence over a complex
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/elliptic+curve"} elliptic curve
            = succeed "-equivariant" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/conformal+blocks"} conformal blocks
            of the WZW model over the given elliptic curve. (See also below at
            = succeed ")." do
              %em
                %a{:href => "#RelationToConformalBlocks"} Properties – Relation to conformal blocks
          %p
            Next, by the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/holographic+principle"} holographic principle
            of the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/AdS3-CFT2+and+CS-WZW+correspondence"} 3dCS/2dWZW-correspondence
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/conformal+blocks"} conformal blocks
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/WZW+model"} WZW model
            on a surface is identified with the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/space+of+quantum+states"} space of quantum states
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Chern-Simons+theory"} Chern-Simons theory
            over that surface. This in turn, by the general rules of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+quantization"} geometric quantization
            and specifically by the discussion at
            = succeed "," do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantization+of+3d+Chern-Simons+theory"} quantization of 3d Chern-Simons theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/holomorphic+sections"} holomorphic sections
            of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/prequantum+line+bundle"} prequantum line bundle
            over the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/moduli+space+of+flat+connections"} moduli space of flat connections
            (
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            \-
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/principal+connections"}> principal connections
            )
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi M
                    %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M_G
            over the given elliptic curve. And
            %em that
            is indeed what
            = succeed "-equivariant" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
          %p
            In other words, universal
            = succeed "-equivariant" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            = succeed ")," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/moduli+space+of+elliptic+curves"} moduli space of elliptic curves
            %em
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
              \-equivariant
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tmf"} tmf
            of the point, is essentially the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/modular+functor"} modular functor
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/3d+Chern-Simons+theory"} 3d Chern-Simons theory
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#Lurie"} Lurie 09, remark 5.2
          %p
            But observe that actually it is a bit more: a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/modular+functor"} modular functor
            assigns just an abstract vector space to a surface, which however is meant to be obtained by the process of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantization"} quantization
            of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/3d+Chern-Simons+theory"} 3d Chern-Simons theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/prequantum+line+bundle"} prequantum line bundle
            (over
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/phase+space"} phase space
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/moduli+space+of+flat+connections"} moduli space of flat connections
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi M
                    %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M_G
            on the given elliptic curve). (Beware that, while this is true over the complex numbers, as discussed
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "moduli+space+of+connections#FlatConnectionsOverATorus"} here
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "http://mathoverflow.net/a/226716/381"} here
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantization"} quantization
            process and not just the resulting
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/space+of+quantum+states"} space of quantum states
            in that it actually assigns to an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/elliptic+curve"} elliptic curve
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            and suitable
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+group"} Lie group
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            that
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/prequantum+line+bundle"} prequantum line bundle
            over the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/moduli+space+of+elliptic+curves"} moduli space of elliptic curves
            (or equivalently its
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sheaf"} sheaf
            of sections). Notice that this
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/local+prequantum+field+theory"} pre-quantum
            information is crucial for deep aspects in the context of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/3d+Chern-Simons+theory"} 3d Chern-Simons theory
            and the 2d
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Wess-Zumino-Witten+model"} Wess-Zumino-Witten model
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/holographic+principle"} holographic relation
            that identifies the latter as the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/boundary+field+theory"} boundary field theory
            of the former (explicitly so by the
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/FRS-theorem+on+rational+2d+CFT"} FRS-theorem on rational 2d CFT
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/3d+TQFT"} 3d TQFT
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantization"} quantization
            by choosing
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/polarizations"} polarizations
            in the form of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/conformal+structures"} conformal structures
            on the elliptic curves, such as to be actually identified with a space of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/conformal+blocks"} conformal blocks
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Reshetikhin-Turaev+construction"} Reshetikhin-Turaev construction
            of the Chern-Simons
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/3d+TQFT"} 3d TQFT
            this information is in a choice of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalence+of+categories"} equivalence
            of the given
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/modular+tensor+category"} modular tensor category
            with the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+of+representations"} category of representations
            of a rational
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vertex+operator+algebra"} vertex operator algebra
          %p
            In summary we have as a
            %strong slogan
            that:
          %ul
            %li
              %em
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi G
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
                \-Equivariant
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi tmf
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} tmf
                over the point is essentially an incarnation of the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/local+prequantum+field+theory"} pre-quantum
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/modular+functor"} modular functor
              of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/3d+Chern-Simons+theory"} 3d G-Chern-Simons theory
              over
              = succeed "-1" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/genus+of+a+surface"} genus
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/elliptic+curves"} elliptic curves
              = succeed "." do
                %em
                  , together with the
                  = succeed "-process" do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantization"} quantization
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/modular+functor"} modular functor
          %p
            Moreover, by the above reasoning via (
            %a{:href => "#Ando07"}> Ando 07
            ) and using the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/AdS3-CFT2+and+CS-WZW+correspondence"} 3dCS/2dWZW holographic correspondence
            we also have the interpretation of
            = succeed "-equivariant" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            = succeed "-equivariant" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            = succeed ":" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/conformal+blocks"} conformal blocks
            of a bundle of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/parameterized+WZW+models"} parameterized WZW models
            over
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Witten+genus"} Witten genus
          %p
            Here all the statements on the QFT/string theory side involve a parameter called the “level”, which is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/characteristic+class"} characteristic class
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/universal+Chern-Simons+circle+3-bundle"} universal Chern-Simons circle 3-bundle
            that is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/prequantum+n-bundle"} prequantum 3-bundle
            governing the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/3d+Chern-Simons+theory"} 3d Chern-Simons theory
            (whose
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/transgression"} transgression
            to the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/moduli+space+of+flat+connections"} moduli space of flat connections
            is the “theta”-
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/prequantum+line+bundle"} prequantum line bundle
            there). On the cohomological side this corresponds to a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/twisted+cohomology"} twist
            of the cohomology theory.
          %p
            Now with equivariant
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi tmf
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} tmf
            identified with the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantization+of+Chern-Simons+theory"} quantization of Chern-Simons theory
            in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dimension"} dimension
            2 this way (the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/modular+functor"} modular functor
            together with its pre-quantum origin via
            = succeed ")," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+quantization"} geometric quantization
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/local+quantum+field+theory"} local quantum field theory
            (“
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/extended+TQFT"}> extended TQFT
            ”) suggests to ask for a refinement of this also to dimensions 1 and 0, such that the higher dimensional data arises by “tracing”/
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/transgression"}> transgression
            \. There is such a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/local+prequantum+field+theory"} local prequantum field theory
            refinement of 3d Chern-Simons theory, governed in dimension 0 by the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/universal+Chern-Simons+circle+3-bundle"} universal Chern-Simons circle 3-bundle
            regarded as a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/prequantum+n-bundle"} prequantum 3-bundle
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/transgression"} transgression
            of that to the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/moduli+space+of+flat+connections"} moduli space of flat connections
            is precisely the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/prequantum+bundle"} prequantum bundle
            over
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi M
                    %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M_G
            that appears in the above discussion (e.g.
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/schreiber/show/Extended+higher+cup-product+Chern-Simons+theories"} FSS 12
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/schreiber/show/A+higher+stacky+perspective+on+Chern-Simons+theory"} FSS 13
          %p
            Now that
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/universal+Chern-Simons+circle+3-bundle"} universal Chern-Simons circle 3-bundle
            in turn is modulated by the geometric refinement of the universal
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/second+Chern+class"} second Chern class
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/first+fractional+Pontryagin+class"} first fractional Pontryagin class
            given by a map of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+infinity-stacks"} smooth infinity-stacks
            of the form
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi B
                    %mi G
                    %mo →
                    %msup
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                        %mi B
                      %mn 3
                    %mi U
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
